**Dependency inversion** says high-level policy should not depend on low-level details; both depend on abstractions. **Dependency injection** is a technique to supply those dependencies from outside.

## A plain TypeScript example

```ts
// Owned by the business layer
export interface OrderRepository {
  save(order: Order): Promise<void>;
  byId(id: string): Promise<Order | null>;
}
export interface PaymentGateway {
  charge(orderId: string, pence: number): Promise<"ok" | "declined">;
}

export class PlaceOrder {
  constructor(
    private readonly orders: OrderRepository,
    private readonly payments: PaymentGateway,
  ) {}

  async execute(order: Order) {
    const result = await this.payments.charge(order.id, order.totalPence);
    if (result === "declined") throw new Error("Payment declined");
    await this.orders.save(order);
  }
}

// composition root (server start-up)
const useCase = new PlaceOrder(new MongoOrderRepository(db), new StripePaymentGateway(cfg));
```

