## Why it helps

| Benefit | Explanation |
|---|---|
| Testing | Pass an in-memory fake instead of MongoDB or a network call |
| Replaceability | Change provider by writing a new adapter |
| Clear boundaries | Business code has no imports from drivers or SDKs |

## Containers or frameworks

Libraries such as Nest, tsyringe or Awilix automate wiring. They are useful in large codebases but optional. Start with manual wiring in a **single composition root**; add a container when wiring becomes painful.

## Common mistakes

- Creating an interface for everything, even stable, simple code.
- Letting the interface mirror the library's API instead of the business need.
- Instantiating dependencies inside classes with new, which defeats injection.
- Using a global service locator that hides dependencies.

Example (hypothetical): swapping a payment provider means writing one new adapter and changing one line in the composition root.
